rsive deep models for semantic compositionality over a sentiment treebank. In Proceedings of the 2013 conference on empirical methods in natural language processing, Cited by: cite92†§6 .\nL347:   * Sun et al. (2023) X. Sun, X. Li, Y. Meng, X. Ao, L. Lyu, J. Li, and T. Zhang Defending against backdoor attacks in natural language generation. In Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence, Cited by: cite89†§1 , cite88†§3 , cite90†§4.1 , cite87†§6 .\nL348:   * Touvron et al. (2023) H. Touvron, T. Lavril, G. Izacard, X. Martinet, M. Lachaux, T. Lacroix, B. Rozière, N. Goyal, E. Hambro, F. Azhar, et al. Llama: open and efficient foundation language models. arXiv preprint arXiv:2302.13971. Cited by: cite87†§6 .\nL349:   * Wang et al. (2018) A. Wang, A. Singh, J. Michael, F. Hill, O. Levy, and S. R. Bowman GLUE: a multi-task benchmark and analysis platform for natural language understanding. arXiv preprint arXiv:1804.07461. Cited by: cite92†§6 .\nL350:   * Xu et al. (2022) L. Xu, Y. Chen, G. Cui, H. Gao, and Z. Liu Exploring the universal vulnerability of prompt-based learning paradigm. In Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: NAACL 2022, M. Carpuat, M. de Marneffe, and I. V. Meza Ruiz (Eds.), Cited by: cite88†§3 .\nL351:   * Zhao et al. (2025a) S. Zhao, L. Gan, Z. Guo, X. Wu, Y. Jia, L. Xiao, C. Nguyen, and L. A. Tuan Breaking peft limitations: leveraging weak-to-strong knowledge transfer for backdoor attacks in llms. External Links: cite100†Link Cited by: cite89†§1 .\nL352:   * Zhao et al. (2024a) S. Zhao, L. Gan, Z. Guo, X. Wu, L. Xiao, X. Xu, C. Nguyen, and L. A. Tuan Weak-to-strong backdoor attack for large language models. arXiv preprint arXiv:2409.17946. Cited by: cite85†§2 .\nL353:   * Zhao et al. (2024b) S. Zhao, L. Gan, A. T. Luu, J. Fu, L. Lyu, M. Jia, and J. Wen Defending against weight-poisoning backdoor attacks for parameter-efficient fine-tuning. In Findings of NAACL 2024, K. Duh, H. Gomez, and S. Bethard (Eds.), Cited by: cite96†§A.1 , cite101†§A.1 , cite89†§1 , cite97†§2 , cite88†§3 , cite90†§4.1 , cite99†§4.1 , cite84†§6 .\nL354:   * Zhao et al. (2025b) S. Zhao, X. Wu, C. T. Nguyen, Y. Jia, M. Jia, F. Yichao, and A. T. Luu Unlearning backdoor attacks for LLMs with weak-to-strong knowledge distillation. In Findings of ACL 2025, W. Che, J. Nabende, E. Shutova, and M. T. Pilehvar (Eds.), Cited by: cite96†§A.1 , cite89†§1 , cite97†§2 , cite90†§4.1 , cite99†§4.1 .\nL355:   * Zhou et al. (2024) X. Zhou, J. Li, T. Zhang, L. Lyu, M. Yang, and J. He Backdoor attacks with input-unique triggers in nlp. In Joint European Conference on Machine Learning and Knowledge Discovery in Databases, Cited by: cite85†§2 .\nL356: ## Appendix A Appendix\nL357: ### A.1 Implementation Details\nL358: \nL359: Training resources. All experiments were conducted on two different Ubuntu Linux machines: one equipped with an NVIDIA RTX 5060 Ti GPU with 16GB memory and the other with an NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU with 24GB memory.\nL360: Poisoned pre-training hyperparameters. We use the AdamW optimizer with a learning rate of $2\\times 10^{-5}$ and a weight decay of $0.01$. A linear learning-rate scheduler is applied, with the number of warm-up steps set to 6% of the total training steps. The batch size is set to 32 for BERT and RoBERTa models and 4 for LLaMA models. All poisoned pre-training runs are configured so that the ASR exceeds 95%. The number of training epochs is 3.\nL361: Fine-tuning hyperparameters. The learning rate is selected from $\\{2\\times 10^{-5},2\\times 10^{-4},2\\times 10^{-3}\\}$ to ensure convergence. We apply a linear learning-rate scheduler with 6% warm-up steps and fix the weight decay to 0.01. The batch size is set to 32. For LoRA, we set the rank $r=8$ for BERT and RoBERTa and $r=16$ for LLaMA, with scaling $\\alpha=16$ and dropout rate $0.1$. All these settings follow cite36†Zhao et al. (2024b) ; cite37†Zhao et al. (2025b) .\nL362: The total number of fine-tuning epochs is 20 for BERT and RoBERTa and 5 for LLaMA. For PiSSA, DoRA, and OLoRA, we use the default hyperparameters provided by the PEFT library (cite102†Mangrulkar et al., 2022 ). For RoRA, the clean-strengthened regularization dropout rate $p$ is selected via grid search over $\\{0.05,0.1,0.15,0.2,0.3\\}$, and $p=0.1$ is used in most experiments to ensure training stability.\nL363: The trigger-insensitive regularization strength $\\lambda$ is tuned over $\\{1,5,10,15,20\\}$, with $\\lambda=10$ adopted in most cases. Post-training spectral rescaling is applied to the top three layers of the model, which primarily govern the classification decision boundary. The results of these grid-search experiments are reported in Section cite20†6.2 .\nL364: Evaluation metrics. Regarding evaluation metrics, clean accuracy (CA) represents the classification accuracy on the clean test set, while attack success rate (ASR) is measured on the poisoned test set.\nL365: Evaluation results. In Table cite76†2 , the results for FFT, LoRA, Back-Translation, SCPD, and ONION are taken directly from cite36†Zhao et al. (2024b) . All other baselines, as well as our RoRA implementation, are produced on our own, and the reported values correspond to the best performance across multiple runs. Unless otherwise noted, results in the remaining tables and figures also reflect the best performance from our repeated experiments.\nL366: Model  | Attack  | Method  | SST-2  | CR\nL367: CA$\\uparrow$  | ASR$\\downarrow$  | $\\Delta\\uparrow$  | CA$\\uparrow$  | ASR$\\downarrow$  | $\\Delta\\uparrow$\nL368: RoBERTa  | BadNet  | LoRA  | 95.71  | 99.74  | -4.03  | 92.26  | 99.93  | -7.67\nL369: +RoRA (Ours)  | 95.99  | 6.49  | 89.50  | 93.81  | 7.28  | 86.53\nL370: PiSSA  | 95.06  | 84.82  | 10.24  | 92.90  | 99.79  | -6.89\nL371: +RoRA (Ours)  | 95.39  | 8.80  | 86.59  | 94.06  | 31.39  | 62.67\nL372: DoRA  | 95.61  | 66.23  | 29.38  | 92.65  | 100.0  | -7.35\nL373: +RoRA (Ours)  | 96.38  | 6.16  | 90.22  | 93.16  | 4.99  | 88.17\nL374: OLoRA  | 95.72  | 71.84  | 23.88  | 93.03  | 91.89  | 1.14\nL375: +RoRA (Ours)  | 95.66  | 6.49  | 89.17  | 93.94  | 14.97  | 78.97\nL376: InSent  | LoRA  | 95.68  | 87.09  | 8.59  | 92.69  | 98.82  | -6.13\nL377: +RoRA (Ours)  | 95.83  | 17.05  | 78.78  | 92.90  | 4.78  | 88.12\nL378: PiSSA  | 95.77  | 97.25  | -1.48  | 92.13  | 97.30  | -5.17\nL379: +RoRA (Ours)  | 95.88  | 32.34  | 63.54  | 92.90  | 31.39  | 61.51\nL380: DoRA  | 95.99  | 99.34  | -3.35  | 92.39  | 94.39  | -2.00\nL381: +RoRA (Ours)  | 95.33  | 21.56  | 73.77  | 91.61  | 7.90  | 83.71\nL382: OLoRA  | 95.83  | 97.69  | -1.86  | 93.16  | 54.89  | 38.27\nL383: +RoRA (Ours)  | 95.06  | 14.63  | 80.43  | 93.16  | 11.64  | 81.52\nL384: LLaMA  | BadNet  | LoRA  | 95.94  | 100.0  | -4.06  | 92.39  | 100.0  | -7.61\nL385: +RoRA (Ours)  | 93.74  | 2.31  | 91.43  | 92.77  | 4.37  | 88.40\nL386: PiSSA  | 95.61  | 98.68  | -3.07  | 92.26  | 99.38  | -7.12\nL387: +RoRA (Ours)  | 95.72  | 32.89  | 62.83  | 92.26  | 66.74  | 25.52\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSkyNomad: On Using Multi-Region Spot Instances to Minimize AI Batch Job Cost (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.06520v1)\nciteturn26949view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn26941view3\",\"lineno\":325}); Total lines: 554\nL281: where $\\eta_{s}=\\max(0,\\bar{L}_{s}-d)/\\bar{L}_{s}$ is the effectiveness of candidate state $s$, i.e., the fraction of its lifetime spent doing useful work. $V(t)\\cdot\\eta_{s}$ thus denotes the effective value of the progress. Short-lived spot instances have lower effectiveness, since cold-start overhead dominates their runtime. The second and third terms capture the operational cost of running in $s$, namely the compute cost combined with the amortized egress cost.\nL282: The migration cost $E_{r_{0}\\to r}$ is amortized over the lifetime, so longer-lived instances amortize this fixed cost more effectively.\nL283: Special cases.  For on-demand instances, we assume $\\bar{L}_{(r,\\texttt{od})}\\to\\infty$ (no preemption), so $\\eta_{(r,\\texttt{od})}\\to 1$ and the migration cost is fully amortized, giving $U_{(r,\\texttt{od})}=V(t)-C_{(r,\\texttt{od})}$. For idling, no cost is incurred but no progress is made, so $U_{(r,\\texttt{idle})}=0$. This means running on $s$ is worthwhile only when $U>0$, i.e., when the effective value of progress exceeds its cost.\nL284: ### 4.7 Putting it All Together\nL285: \nL286: Algorithm 1 SkyNomad Scheduling Policy\nL287: \nL288: 1: Input: Job requiring total progress $P$, deadline $T$, and candidate regions $\\mathcal{R}$\nL289: \nL290: 2: $(r_{0},m_{0})\\leftarrow($initial region, idle$)$\nL291: \nL292: 3: while $p(t)<P$ do $\\triangleright$ Every scheduling step\nL293: \nL294: 4:   if SafetyNet(t) then $\\triangleright$ cite19†section 4.2 L295: \nL296: 5:    Launch od if $m\\neq\\texttt{od}$; continue\nL297: \nL298: 6:   ProbeRegions($\\mathcal{R}$) periodically $\\triangleright$ cite20†section 4.3 L299: 7:   $V\\leftarrow\\textsc{ProgressValueEstim}(t,T,p(t),P)$ $\\triangleright$ cite24†section 4.5 L300: \nL301: 8:   for each state $s\\in\\mathcal{R}\\times\\{\\texttt{spot},\\texttt{od},\\texttt{idle}\\}$ do\nL302: \nL303: 9:    $\\bar{L}_{s}\\leftarrow\\textsc{PredictLifetime}(s)$ $\\triangleright$ cite21†section 4.4 L304: \nL305: 10:    $U_{s}\\leftarrow\\textsc{CalcUtility}(s,V,\\bar{L}_{s})$ $\\triangleright$ cite25†section 4.6 L306: \nL307: 11:   Sort candidate states by $U_{s}$ descending\nL308: 12:   for each $s=(r,m)$ with $U_{s}>U_{(r_{0},m_{0})}$ do $\\triangleright$ cite26†section 4.7 L309: \nL310: 13:    succeeds $\\leftarrow$ Launch $s\\textbf{ or }m=\\texttt{idle}$\nL311: \nL312: 14:    if succeeds then\nL313: \nL314: 15:      Terminate $(r_{0},m_{0})$ if $m_{0}\\neq\\texttt{idle}$\nL315: \nL316: 16:      $(r_{0},m_{0})\\leftarrow(r,m)$; break\nL317: \nL318: 17: Terminate $(r_{0},m_{0})$ $\\triangleright$ Terminate on job completion\nL319: cite90†Algorithm 1 summarizes the scheduling policy. The core idea is simple: at each scheduling step, evaluate all candidates and migrate to any option that is better than the current state. The scheduler first applies the safety net when needed (cite19†section 4.2 , lines cite94†4 –cite95†5 ). It then periodically sends lightweight probes to all regions (cite20†section 4.3 , line cite96†6 ).\nL320: After computing the value of future progress based on the current deadline pressure (cite24†section 4.5 , line cite97†7 ), the policy iterates over all candidate states $s=(r,m)$, predicts their lifetimes using the virtual instance view (cite21†section 4.4 , line cite98†9 ), and computes their utility $U_{(r,m)}$ (cite25†section 4.6 , line cite99†10 ).\nL321: It then compares each candidate against the current state’s utility $U_{(r_{0},m_{0})}$.^{2}^{2} 2 In practice, we add a hysteresis threshold $\\Delta$ to prevent thrashing: the scheduler switches only when $U_{(r,m)}>U_{(r_{0},m_{0})}+\\Delta$. Candidates are attempted in descending utility order, and the scheduler migrates to the first one that succeeds (idling always succeeds; spot may fail due to unavailability).\nL322: This approach ensures the system continually moves toward better options as conditions change.\nL323: ## 5 SkyNomad Implementation\nL324: We implemented SkyNomad , a general deadline-aware batch job execution system that combines spot and on-demand instances across multiple regions to guarantee deadline completion while minimizing cost. SkyNomad is workload-agnostic, supporting any workload that periodically checkpoints to a persistent store, and it seamlessly manages checkpoint migration when moving across regions.\nL325: SkyNomad is built on top of SkyPilot [cite100†54 ], an open-source multi-cloud system, and adds a scheduler on top of it with ~6,000 lines of code. The implementation supports common training frameworks including PyTorch [cite77†57 ] and Hugging Face Transformers [cite101†48 ].\nL326: Scheduler.  The scheduler manages the entire lifecycle of the job. It first performs periodic probes to collect the data used in (cite20†section 4.3 ). The probe interval is configurable and is set to 2 hours by default, which we find sufficient to capture key characteristics of the spot market while keeping probing overhead reasonable (cite29†section 6.1 ). The scheduler then forwards the probe results to the policy and provisions instances according to the policy’s decision.\nL327: Checkpoint migration.  SkyNomad assumes that the workload periodically checkpoints to a persistent store (e.g., a cloud bucket) and it handles checkpoint migration when a job is moved to another region. Because data on public clouds must be loaded onto an instance after it is provisioned, SkyNomad implements a two-stage pipeline to accelerate migration. While the target instance is provisioning, SkyNomad first copies the checkpoint to an object store in the target region.\nL328: Once the instance becomes available, SkyNomad downloads the checkpoint during runtime setup so that the job can resume execution promptly.\nL329: (a) 4$\\times$L4 (g6.12xlarge)\nL330: \nL331: (b) 8$\\times$A100 (p4d.24xlarge)\nL332: \nL333: (c) 4$\\times$A10G (g5.12xlarge)\nL334: Figure 6: End-to-end cost comparison across three accelerator types. SkyNomad achieves the lowest cost in all configurations. Single-region baselines (UP and ASM) show high variance depending on regional spot availability. Only multi-region approaches incur egress costs (dotted region at bar tops). A100 shows minimal egress because the large checkpoint size (500 GB) makes cross-region migration expensive, so the system prefers intra-region zone switches. All jobs complete within their deadlines.\nL335: ## 6 Evaluation\nL336: \nL337: We evaluate SkyNomad comprehensively both on real cloud deployments that experience actual preemptions and in simulation using replayed spot traces. We seek to answer the following questions:\nL338: \nL339:   * •\nL340: \nL341: Can SkyNomad reduce cost in real cloud environments while still completing jobs within their deadlines? (cite29†section 6.1 )\nL342: \nL343:   * •\nL344: \nL345: Can SkyNomad generalize across different accelerator types and their preemption patterns? (cite29†section 6.1 and cite32†section 6.2.2 )\nL346: \nL347:   * •\nL348: Can SkyNomad achieve consistently low cost under varying deadline tightness, number of regions, and checkpoint sizes? (cite33†section 6.2.3 to cite35†section 6.2.5 )\nL349: \nL350:   * •\nL351: \nL352: Can SkyNomad generalize across geographic deployments and data sovereignty constraints? (cite36†section 6.2.6 )\nL353: ### 6.1 End-to-End Experiments\nL354: \nL355: We run end-to-end experiments on AWS to fine-tune LLMs using three different accelerator configurations: 4$\\times$L4, 8$\\times$A100, and 4$\\times$A10G. We launch all baseline systems and SkyNomad simultaneously so they experience the same real-time spot preemption events. The total cost of these experiments is approximately $8,000.\nL356: \nL357: Baselines.  We compare SkyNomad with:\nL358: \nL359:   * •\nL360: Uniform Progress (UP) [cite62†50 ]: A single-region deadline-aware policy that distributes progress evenly over time and uses on-demand and spot instances interchangeably to guarantee deadline completion.\nL361: \nL362:   * •\nL363: AWS SageMaker Managed Spot (ASM) [cite59†4 ]: A production-grade batch job execution system that uses spot instances to reduce cost. ASM relies exclusively on spot instances and can draw spot capacity from multiple availability zones within a single region, but only switches zones upon preemption and does not support multi-region.\nL364: \nL365:   * •\nL366: UP(S): A multi-region extension of UP that we implemented as a baseline, representing SkyPilot’s production failover policy [cite102†41 ]. Upon preemption, it Switches the job to a different region to avoid volatile periods [cite58†32 ], trying candidate regions sequentially from cheapest to most expensive until one succeeds.\nL367: Between preemptions, it follows UP’s progress distribution strategy to guarantee deadline completion and exploit rule [cite62†50 ], staying in the current region as long as it remains available. ^{3}^{3} 3 We also design additional heuristics (UP(A), UP(AP)) to ablate individual components of SkyNomad in cite30†section 6.2 .\nL368: We run both UP and ASM separately in each of three regions (us-west-2, eu-central-1, and ap-northeast-1) to study how regional spot availability affects their performance. ASM is not inherently deadline-aware; to ensure a fair comparison, we manually trigger the safety net (cite19†section 4.2 ), switching to on-demand when needed to meet the deadline.\nL369: Workloads.  We use Hugging Face Transformers [cite101†48 ] to fine-tune Qwen3 models [cite103†52 ] on the Orca-Math dataset [cite104†35 ]: Qwen3-4B on 4$\\times$L4 (g6.12xlarge) and 4$\\times$A10G (g5.12xlarge), and Qwen3-14B on 8$\\times$A100 (p4d.24xlarge). Each job runs on a single instance and requires about 30 hours of compute with a 45-hour deadline. Checkpoint sizes are 100 GB and 500 GB, respectively.\nL370: Cold start time, including instance provisioning, environment setup, and checkpoint loading, is approximately 6 minutes.\nL371: Results.  cite105†Figure 6 shows the total cost for each system across the three accelerator configurations. SkyNomad achieves the lowest cost in all cases: 10–55% savings versus the best single-region baseline, 47–69% versus the average single-region baseline, and 4–44% versus the multi-region baseline UP(S).\nL372: These savings already account for SkyNomad ’s overheads: egress costs for cross-region migrations account for up to 16% of total cost (dotted portion in cite105†Figure 6 ), while probing costs are negligible at $1–3 per job. Despite the egress overhead, cross-region migration enables SkyNomad to exploit cheaper spot capacity that would otherwise be inaccessible.\nL373: Single region solutions are inherently limited.  Single-region baselines face two limitations. First, the ever-changing nature of the spot market means no single region consistently offers the best availability or price. A region that performs well at the start of a job may become unavailable mid-execution, and static region selection cannot adapt to such changes.\nL374: In our experiments across three representative regions, UP’s total cost varies by up to 1.7$\\times$ on L4 (cite106†Figure 6(a) ) and 1.8$\\times$ on A100 (cite107†Figure 6(b) ). While the best region eu-central-1 for L4 (cite106†Figure 6(a) ) achieves low cost with abundant spot capacity, the worst-performing region (ap-northeast-1) has no spot available for more than 70% of the time and must fall back to expensive on-demand instances, driving 74% of its total cost.\nL375: ASM shows even higher variance (up to 2.8$\\times$ on A10G) because its zone-level failover may land on a more expensive zone (cite108†Figure 6(c) ). Second, spot availability patterns differ across accelerator types: eu-central-1 performs well for L4 but poorly for A100, while us-west-2 shows the opposite pattern. As a result, region choices informed by one GPU type do not necessarily transfer to another.\nL376: Given these limitations, even the best-performing single-region baseline (UP in eu-central-1) costs 10% more than SkyNomad .\nL377: Naive multi-region is not enough.  Multi-region approaches achieve lower and more stable costs by drawing spot capacity from multiple regions; both SkyNomad and UP(S) benefit from the aggregated spot resources across regions and complete the job without using any on-demand instances (cite106†Figure 6(a) ). However, UP(S) only migrates when preempted. If it lands in an expensive region that happens to have stable availability, it stays there indefinitely.\n"
